<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_solving-forces2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Using Action-Reaction Pairs</title>

  <subsubsection>
	<title>Practice Activities</title>
</subsubsection>

<activity xml:id="class_book-stack">
	<title>The Book Stack</title>
<introduction>
<p></p>
    <p>
      A stack of two books is at rest on a table.
    </p>
		</introduction>

		<figure xml:id="for_book-stack-0">
			<title>Book Stack Figure</title>
		  <caption>A stack of two books.</caption>
      <image source="BookStack.png" width = "35%">
			<description>Book 2 (blue) on top of book 1 (orange) on a flat table.</description>
			</image>
		</figure>
	<p>
		Use <xref ref="force-analysis" text="title"/> to identify all Action-Reaction (Newton's 3rd Law) Pairs and identify any 
		forces that are equal in magnitude.
	</p>
</activity>
	


<activity xml:id="class_block-race">
	<title>The Block Race</title>
<introduction>
<p></p>
    <p>
		Block A is accelerated across a frictionless table by a hanging <m>10 \mathrm{~N}</m> mass. 
		An identical block B is accelerated by a constant <m>10 \mathrm{~N}</m> tension in the string.
    </p>
	<figure xml:id="fig_block-race">
		<title>Block Race Figure</title>
	  <caption>Two Blocks connected to strings.</caption>
  <image source="BlocksRace.png" width = "100%">
		<description>Left: Block A connected to a string which runs over a pulley to a hanging block with a 1 kg hanging mass with gravitational force of about 10 N.  Right: Block B connected to a string which runs over a pulley to a hand that acts a constant downward tension of about 10 N.</description>
		</image>
</figure>
		</introduction>
	<task>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Before you begin, predict which block you think will have a larger acceleration.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</task>
	<task>
	<p> 
		Use <xref ref="force-analysis" text="title"/> to determine the acceleration of each block.  
		Sketching free-body diagrams for each object is essential!
	</p>
	</task>	
</activity>

<project xml:id="class_block-pair">
	<title>The Pair of Blocks</title>
		<statement>
			<p></p>
    <p>
		Blocks A and B are connected by an ideal string via a massless pulley.  The coefficient of kinetic friction is <m>\mu</m>.
    </p>
	<figure xml:id="fig_block-pair">
		<title>Pair of Blocks Figure</title>
	  <caption>Two Blocks connected by a string over a pulley.</caption>
  <image source="BlockPulleyFriction.png" width = "60%">
		<description>Block A connected to a string which runs over a pulley to a hanging block.</description>
		</image>
</figure>
			<p>
				Use the <xref ref="fig_realworld"/> to determine the acceleration of each block.
			</p>
			<p>
				This situation is a particularly good one for special-case analysis: what are some cases you might want to try?
			</p>
		</statement>

	</project>

<project xml:id="class_pulley-pair">
	<title>The Pair of Pulleys</title>
		<statement>
			<p></p>
    <p>
		Blocks A and B are connected by an ideal string via two massless pulleys.
    </p>
	<figure xml:id="fig_pulley-pair">
		<title>Pair of Pulleys Figure</title>
	  <caption>Two Blocks connected by a string to two pulleys.</caption>
  <image source="BlockPulley2.png" width = "40%">
		<description>Block A connected to a string which runs over two pulleys before being connected to the ceiling.  Block B is suspended from the second pulley.</description>
		</image>
</figure>
			<p>
				Use the <xref ref="fig_realworld"/> to determine the acceleration of each block.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<hint>
			<p>
				The magnitudes of the blocks' accelerations are different.  How can you relate them?
			</p>
		</hint>
	</project>



	
<!--NO AI-->
</section>

